<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The "glass" action controls overlaying the card photo (item_match_card).

    A near-copy of bg_v2_glass_pill, kept SEPARATE on purpose. That drawable
    is also used by item_explore_nearby, where the chip sits on a themed
    surface and its light-mode fill is correct as-is. Making the shared
    drawable fixed-translucent-white would have turned that chip into white
    on white in light mode.

    What differs here: the fill and border are fixed light in both themes.
    The shared version resolves to an OPAQUE #EFEFEF in light mode, which on
    a photo is not glass at all but a solid grey block — and, worse, it was
    the reason the dark icons on it still read, so the icon tints on this
    card could not be fixed without fixing this at the same time.

    The design specifies a backdrop blur behind a 10% white fill. Android has
    no backdrop blur in a shape drawable (RenderEffect is API 31+, and this
    app's minSdk is 24), so this approximates it with the translucent fill
    alone. The photo shows through un-softened; the control still reads as a
    distinct surface because the fill and hairline border both survive.
-->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="rectangle">
    <solid android:color="@color/wedora_glass_on_photo" />
    <corners android:radius="16dp" />
    <stroke android:width="1dp" android:color="@color/wedora_hairline_on_photo" />
</shape>
